#!/bin/sh
# Attach to a cm session, creating it if needed.
#
# kitty's save_as_session records a window's launch argv, not the process actually running in it. Going
# through this wrapper keeps the session name in the argv (as $1) so restoring the session reattaches
# instead of creating a new one, and keeps the saved path stable rather than baking in a versioned
# install path that resolving `cm` directly would produce.
#
# No `unset CM_SESSION` here, unlike the zmx version. zmx treats that variable as a request to retarget
# an existing client, so an inherited value would hijack whichever window is already showing that
# session; cm only ever exports CM_SESSION and never reads it, so attaching from inside a session
# creates a nested one, which is what asking for it should do.
#
# No SIGWINCH nudge either. That existed because zmx delivers no signal on a same-size reattach and runs
# no new shell, so a restored window kept kitty's fallback title until something was typed. cm re-emits
# OSC 2 as part of the screen it restores, and nudges the pty itself when a client attaches at the size
# the session already has.
# Deliberately not --own.
#
# --own ends the session when its client disconnects without detaching, which sounds right for a
# per-window session and is wrong here: quitting kitty disconnects every client, so it would destroy
# every session rather than leave them to be restored, which is the entire point of this setup. The
# cm_reap.py watcher handles the deliberate-close case instead, because it can tell a closed window from
# kitty going away and --own cannot.
exec cm attach "$1"
